course: dictionary
lesson: 2662
title: Devolution: sharing out power
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Devolution» is a word about power. Context clues help you work it out: «Devolution means parts of the UK, like Scotland and Wales, make some of their own decisions, including about health.» The parts are Scotland and Wales, and they make their own decisions.
explain: So devolution means power is shared out from the central government to smaller parts of the country, so those parts can decide some things for themselves. Check by swapping your meaning into the sentence: «Scotland and Wales make some of their own decisions» still makes sense.
explain: The word has other meanings too. In a formal sense, devolution means passing a job, duty or property on to someone else. Always check which meaning fits the sentence around it.
fact: Devolution means handing some powers from a central government to smaller regional governments or parliaments.
fact: Devolution gave Scotland its own Parliament in 1999.
fact: Wales has the Senedd, and Northern Ireland has its own Assembly, and they make laws on things like schools and health.
fact: The UK Parliament in Westminster keeps other powers, such as defence.
fact: In a formal sense, devolution means passing a job, duty or property on to someone else.
exercise: «Devolution means parts of the UK, like Scotland and Wales, make some of their own decisions.» What does devolution mean? | All decisions are made in one place, in London | Countries leave the UK altogether | *Some powers are handed to smaller regional governments | Living things change into simpler forms
exercise: In 1999, devolution gave Scotland its own what? | Passport office | Army | Football club | *Parliament
exercise: Which power does the UK Parliament in Westminster keep? | Local bins | *Defence | Schools | Health
test: Which word means passing a job or duty to someone else? | Division | *Devolution | Dismissal | Decoration
test: What do the USA, Canada and Australia have that is like devolution? | One king for every region | No laws at all | *States or provinces with their own powers | A single Parliament for the whole country
exam: Which phrase is a real example of this word? | devolved weather | *devolved government | devolved holiday | devolved dinner
